  PASS  capture file exists and is EMPTY before any request (a missing file would mean the recorder never started)
  PASS  Anthropic /v1/messages answered with a valid message object
  PASS  OpenAI /v1/chat/completions answered with a valid completion object
  PASS  the STREAMING reply terminates -- a stub that never ends its own event stream hangs the client and reads as a client that never arrived
  PASS  3 requests sent -> 3 captured (a recorder that drops one reports a cheaper client than the real one)
  PASS  the captured body is VERBATIM equal to what was sent
  PASS  raw_bytes equals the exact serialized length on the wire
  PASS  raw_bytes is the OBSERVED length (113 B of compact JSON), not a re-serialisation of the parsed body (126 B) -- the two differ here on purpose, because comparing them to each other cannot tell them apart
  PASS  an UNPARSEABLE body is still recorded, marked `__unparseable__`, with its real wire length (38 B) -- dropping it or storing an empty body would make the request vanish into the probe count downstream
  PASS  6 requests sent -> 6 recorded, including the two the recorder could not parse (a recorder that writes only what it understood reports a cheaper client than the real one)
  PASS  a request that carried NO payload is recorded with `body: null`, not under the unparseable marker -- nothing sent is not the same as could not parse, and conflating them made the instrument refuse a whole good capture
  PASS  header NAMES recorded, header VALUES not -- the Authorization value sent above is absent from the capture
  PASS  both protocols landed on one capture, each tagged with its endpoint
  PASS  the arm label is carried onto every record
  PASS  a CHUNKED POST is de-chunked and recorded with its real length (387 B in 2 chunks, no Content-Length header at all) -- it used to record `raw_bytes: 0, body: null` and be counted downstream as a startup probe
  PASS  a body this recorder could not FRAME is marked `__unread__` with a NULL length and the reason -- not `raw_bytes: 0`, which would read as an observation that nothing was sent
  PASS  a NON-ASCII body keeps its exact UTF-8 length (2201 B, not the 801 characters it decodes to) and round-trips unchanged -- decoding it as ascii replaces every multibyte character and inflated one real payload 2.9x

self-test: 17 pass, 0 fail
exit=0
